  "step_3": "The decision reason involves adjusting and weighting low-probability outcomes (which are present in this problem) and comparing the expected disutilities under this transformation. However, the individual did not engage in any weighting or adjustment of probabilities; instead, they effectively ignored the low-probability losses altogether, treating them as irrelevant. The justification centers on dismissing the improbable losses rather than transforming and evaluating them more heavily, as the decision reason prescribes. In fact, the individual’s approach is closer to probability neglect or simplification, whereas the decision reason prescribes a more systematic overweighting of rare events. There is no indication that the individual computed or considered adjusted weights or summed weighted outcomes. Therefore, while both the reason and the individual focus on low-probability events, the cognitive process—neglect versus systematic overweighting—is fundamentally different.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

